  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Consider the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ \\sqrt{k} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Given that $u\\cdot v=-20$ and $\u2016v\u2016=5$,  what is the smallest possible value of $k?$",
  "worked_solution": "We will use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality,\n\n$$\n|u\\cdot v|\\le \u2016u\u2016\\cdot \u2016v\u2016.\n$$\n\nWe are given all the quantities in the inequality above, except for $\u2016u\u2016.$ So first, we compute $\u2016u\u2016$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016u\u2016 & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{k})^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14+k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le \u2016u\u2016\\cdot \u2016v\u2016 \\\\ |-20| & \\le \\sqrt{14+k}\\cdot (5) \\\\ 4 & \\le \\sqrt{14+k} \\\\ {4}^{2} & \\le {(\\sqrt{14+k})}^{2} \\\\ 16 & \\le 14+k \\\\ 2 & \\le k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $k$ is $2.$",
